From 1177f68a448595bea6fc84e0ee1497128ccf380c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 02:02:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grate: fragment loops are refused like NVIDIA's compiler
 refuses them

The glmark2 loop scene with a uniform bound printed GRATE FRAG TGSI
UNIMPLEMENTED: 0x63 BGNLOOP, which reads like an opcode still to write.
It is not: NVIDIA's fragment compiler (cgtest 1 2 on the same GLSL)
refuses a for loop whose bound is not a constant, error C5013 "profile
does not support "for" statements and "for" could not be unrolled" (and
C5053 for break), while a constant bound is unrolled (5 iterations are
7 instructions, 100 are 102) and a big if/else is flattened (14
instructions, no branch). The fragment unit has no branches, so
refusing the shader is what matching NVIDIA means.

The refusal says so. A comment on the vertex stage's
max_control_flow_depth records why it stays 0 although vp/tgsi.c now
translates loops: with 0 nir_to_tgsi flattens every if, as NVIDIA's
vertex compiler does.

Checked: cgtest 1 2 on floop-glmark, floop-simple, floop-break,
floop-const5, floop-const100 and fif-big (the results above); the
glmark2 loop scene with fragment-uniform=true prints the new line and
is refused as before.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/grate/fp/tgsi.c      | 9 +++++++++
 src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c | 5 +++++
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/grate/fp/tgsi.c b/src/gallium/drivers/grate/fp/tgsi.c
index 340c8f917ac..ffdba0a2b3a 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/grate/fp/tgsi.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/grate/fp/tgsi.c
@@ -1653,6 +1653,15 @@ emit_tgsi_instr(struct grate_fp_shader *fp, const struct tgsi_full_instruction *
       fprintf(stderr, "GRATE FRAG TGSI UNIMPLEMENTED: 0x%02x %s\n",
               inst->Instruction.Opcode,
               tgsi_get_opcode_name(inst->Instruction.Opcode));
+      if (inst->Instruction.Opcode == TGSI_OPCODE_BGNLOOP)
+         /* not a gap to fill: the fragment unit has no branches, and
+          * NVIDIA's compiler refuses the same shader (cgtest 1 2 on a for
+          * loop with a uniform bound: error C5013, profile does not
+          * support "for" statements and "for" could not be unrolled; a
+          * constant bound is unrolled). Matching it means refusing too. */
+         fprintf(stderr, "GRATE FRAG: a loop whose bound is not a constant "
+                         "cannot run on the fragment unit; NVIDIA's compiler "
+                         "refuses it too (C5013), the shader is refused\n");
       fp->allocation_failed = true;
       break;
    }
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c
index 2eda6b86f5b..f36a3c3ac27 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c
@@ -308,6 +308,11 @@ grate_screen_init_shader_caps(struct grate_screen *screen)
    caps->max_alu_instructions = 1024;
    caps->max_tex_instructions = 0;
    caps->max_tex_indirections = 0;
+   /* Stays 0 although vp/tgsi.c translates loops and ifs into BRA: with 0
+    * nir_to_tgsi flattens every if whatever its size (peephole_select limit
+    * ~0), which is what NVIDIA's compiler does (an 8+8 statement if/else is
+    * 87 instructions without a branch, docs/grate-vertex-control-flow.md).
+    * Only loops reach the BRA path. */
    caps->max_control_flow_depth = 0;
    caps->max_inputs = 16;
    caps->max_outputs = 16;
-- 
2.56.0

